LimitPlan shows what a site provides before the package arrives as a battery-limits plan: an isometric drawing of the package footprint inside a dashed battery-limit line, with a numbered square node on each connection keyed to the row of the same number beside it.

- On the site: a `spec` block with `"look": "limit-plan"` (renderer `assets/src/blocks/look-limit-plan.js`), on systems "What your site needs before we arrive" (`#site`). Concept: pass 12 image 220, `design-system/inspiration/gemini/220-p12-systems-site.png`. Not copied from it: its repeated node tags (6 and 7 twice), the generic box skid and the notes column it drops; here one node per row and the drawing is our own package (IsoKit).
- The drawing (IsoKit, light sheet on `surface`, framed in `line-strong`): the battery limit as a `flow` dash-dot line (`16 5 3 5` at 2 px, the same line as RoomLimit) on a `flow-tint` floor; inside it the base frame and the reverse osmosis rack with its local panel; across it the feed water (in) and the drain (out to a floor gully), the power cable through an isolator standing on the line (the cable is drawn with the utilities, the isolator is the isolation point), the product line leaving through a field joint (flange pair on the line) to a tie-in valve on the plant's header (outside, on `surface-sunken`); the tag plate on the rack panel's front; the footprint dimension along the frame.
- Connections (row field `at`, look-only): `limit` (a point on the line), `footprint` (the dimension), `utilities` (the feed crossing in, the drain out to the gully and the power cable), `joint` (the field joint), `tag` (the tag plate), `tiein` (the tie-in valve), `isolation` (the isolator on the line). Default: that order, row by row, which matches the systems block as written. A row whose `at` is unknown, repeated or `"none"` gets no node and a plain number. Where nodes would crowd, a short leader takes the square off the drawing (joint, tag, tie-in, isolator).
- Markup: `section.aq-limit-plan-sec` → `div.aq-limit-plan[data-pk-sticky]` (grid 11/13) → `figure.__fig[data-pk-fig]` (sticky on desktop) > `div.__plan[data-pk-pan]` (`span.__node[data-at][data-pk-key]` HTML squares placed with `isoPct`, first so PhoneKit zooms to the node; `svg.__svg` with `g.__g[data-lp][data-pk-key]` groups, `path.__lead`, `circle.__dot`; `span.__ring`) and `div.__table` (`div.__head`, `ol.__list` of `li.__row[data-at][data-pk-row]`: `span.__no`, `div.__t > h3.__p`, `div.__c` with `ul.__v`, `p.__m`, `p.__go`). `data-pk-key` and `data-pk-row` are the row index; the two footprint groups share theirs.
- Fields: `h`, `intro`, `cols [item, typical, note]` (head labels; the note label is read out before each note), `rows [{p, v, m, link, link_label, at}]`. Values written "a · b" break into lines. `fold` and `fold_noun` are not read: every row shows (S7: the drawing points at every row). Swaps freely with TermsSheet (same fields).
- Nodes and row numbers: 26 px squares (`action` ground, `on-action` mono 12 px), lit in `flow` with `ink-inverse`; a 44 px `flow` ring glides to the lit node.
- Interaction: pointing at or focusing a row or a node lights the row, the node, its leader and the drawing's parts for that connection (`.is-on`, `data-lp-on` on the root), and the ring glides there. Ported from Subframe Motion "Inside the image" (`atlas-hotspots`), MIT, (c) 2026 Subframe: ring 250 ms `cubic-bezier(0.2, 0.8, 0.2, 1)`, node scale 1.12 in 180 ms; changes are immediate under reduced motion.
- Tablets (861–1080 px): the plan on top at full container width, the rows under it.
- **Phones** (≤ 860 px, which includes a 768 tablet's 736 px container): PhoneKit **sticky compact figure** (S8; `data-pk-h="200"`, `data-pk-zoom="1.6"`). The plan becomes a 200 px strip held under the section bar (the plan box keeps the drawing's aspect, `--lp-ar`/`--lp-r` set by the renderer), panned and zoomed to the node of the row on the reading line; nodes and the ring are sized against the zoom (`calc(24px / var(--pk-s))`), so they read at 24 px with 12 px numbers. Every row stays open under the strip (S7: each row has a node pointing at it): node number and item (18 px), the typical value (15 px), the note (14 px, `ink-muted`), the link (44 px). The row on the reading line lights its node, row number and drawing parts, and the ring moves there (`pk:change` → the same `set()` as hover). Without script: the whole plan at full gutter width, then every row open. About 1,560 px at 375 with seven rows (the accordion of build round B's first cut: 1,250, but rows 03–07 lit a node scrolled out of view; the terms-sheet it replaces: 1,005 without a drawing after round A, 2,055 before). Shot tools that screenshot the whole section show the strip pinned over the first row; that is the sticky strip, not a layout fault.
- Use it for site requirements that are physical connections at a battery limit. For package and site responsibilities on either side of the limit, BatteryLimit; for a scope boundary with conditions, ScopeSeam; for commercial terms, TermsSheet.

## Motion

On scroll-in the floor inside the limit fades up and the dash-dot line creeps round once; the package builds the IsoKit way (`AQiso.timeline`: parts back to front, pipes, water); the site's side (cable, isolator, plate, joint, gully) settles in with the pipes; the nodes press onto their connections in row order (0.12 s apart) while the rows rise 8 px. Once, under 4.5 s; nothing runs under reduced motion.
